	"kid": [
		"# A real bike: the Yamaha XJ550",
		"This is a real four-cylinder motorcycle from the early 1980s. Its valves sit under little metal cups (buckets) with thin round discs (shims) on top.",
		"- Each valve needs a TINY gap, about as thick as a sheet of paper, so it can close all the way.",
		"- Too small: the valve can't close and gets burnt. Too big: noisy and a bit less power.",
		"- To fix the gap, you swap the shim for a thinner or thicker one.",
		"> Open the VALVE BENCH, tap NEXT CHECK, and watch which cam bump turns green.",
	],
	"sim": [
		"# Specs (engine cold / room temperature)",
		"- Intake 0.11-0.15 mm (0.004-0.006 in). Exhaust 0.16-0.20 mm (0.006-0.008 in). Same for the air-cooled XJ550/650/750 non-X.",
		"- Shim-over-bucket. Shims are marked Y + thickness x100 (Y270 = 2.70 mm), 0.05 mm per size; fit them number side DOWN. Use Y-marked XJ shims.",
		"- Cylinders 1-4 left to right as you sit on it (#1 by your left knee). Exhaust cam at the front, intake at the back (caps stamped E / I). Firing order 1-2-4-3; one coil fires 1-4, the other 2-3.",
		"# Measuring",
		"- Cover off, plugs out (it turns easily). Turn the crank with a wrench on the bolt in the centre of the ignition rotor under the left cover.",
		"- Measure each valve with its lobe pointing straight away from the shim - where the gap is biggest. A go/no-go feel: the blade slides with a slight drag.",
		"- Record every reading AND the installed shim number before you change anything.",
		"# Changing a shim",
		"- Turn until the lobe holds the bucket fully down, fit the bucket-holding tool (or the zip-tie trick), turn the lobe AWAY from the tool, rotate the bucket's notch to you and pry the shim out.",
		"- New shim = installed + (measured - target). Example: 0.08 mm with a Y205 on an intake (target ~0.13) -> Y200.",
